<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Skule Bårdsson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1189–<a href="/years/1240">1240</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Skule Baardsson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Skule Bårdsson was a Norwegian earl and later duke who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> as regent for the young Haakon IV and was the most powerful noble of his time. Rivalry with Haakon grew, and in 1239 Skule had himself proclaimed king. He was defeated and killed in 1240, and his fall is usually taken to mark the end of the Norwegian civil war era.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/skule-bardsson.jpg" alt="Skule Bårdsson" width="320" height="280" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FinnWikiNo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hertugskule.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Skule was born around 1189 to Bård Guttormsson. Under an arrangement by which he and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway">Haakon Haakonsson</a> shared power, he held much of the real authority in the kingdom. His centre of power was Nidaros, and he had nobles bound to him as liegemen, such as Endrid Bookling and Alf of Leifa-steads, men whose standing probably lay somewhere between a European count and a high <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ministerialis">ministerialis</a>. To ease the rivalry, Skule's elder daughter Margaret Skulesdatter was married to King Haakon in 1225, making the duke the king's father-in-law.</p><p>Believing that he had too little power, Skule joined the opposition to Haakon from time to time. In 1237, in a further attempt at compromise, he was given the first Norwegian title of duke (hertug), but he later took up his rebellion again, and the Icelander writer Snorre Sturlason was among his allies. Skule also clashed with Arnbjorn Jonsson over the Borg stewardship, which Arnbjorn gave up when Abbot Bjorn showed him that the king had granted it to Skule.</p><p>In 1239 his supporters proclaimed him king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> at the Øyrating, the traditional thing of Trøndelag. He tried without success to win over his other son-in-law, Jarl Knut Haakonsson. His party was called the Vårbelgs, after the poor-quality spring pelts worn by poor people. Skule raised an army against Haakon, won a battle at Låka in Nannestad, but was beaten at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In May 1240 Haakon and his supporters defeated Skule. He took refuge in Elgeseter Priory at Nidaros, but Haakon's men burned down the monastery and killed him. His rising was the last phase of the civil wars that had divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> since about 1130, conflicts fed by unclear succession laws, social conditions and the struggle between church and king, and fought in the end between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagler">Bagler</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/birkebeiner">Birkebeiner</a> parties. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henrik-ibsen">Henrik Ibsen</a>'s play Kongs-Emnerne (1863) dramatises the dispute between Duke Skule and King Haakon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Skule Bårdsson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Rissa Municipality, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=63.66,10.04&amp;year=1222" aria-label="Rissa Municipality on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Skule Bårdsson"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5379 4659h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5379 4659h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=63.66,10.04&amp;year=1222">What was here in 1222? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
